> > Looking at your model, the triple-indexing on y[i, j, t]  seems to be the worst offender, in terms of contributing the largest number of nodes to your model:
> >
> >   for(i in 1:nsites){
> >     for(j in 1:nsurveys){
> >       for(t in 1:nyears){
> >         y[i, j, t] ~ dbern(z[i, t]*p)
> >       }
> >     }
> >   }
> >
> > Since each of the nsurveys elements in y[i, 1:nsurveys, t] has an identical distribution, I would suggest using a vectorized bernoulli distribution to jointly calculation the likelihood of the y[i, 1:nsurveys, t] vector.  That should speed up compilation for you.  To help you get started, there are multiple examples of this in the nimbleSCR package <https://github.com/nimble-dev/nimbleSCR>.
