Hi Arjun,

Adopting Kenny's suggestion works on my machine to make MCMC run without problems. I think it makes more sense to scale the matrix fl by row instead of column (by default). So I am using my.data = list(y=y + 1, fl=t(apply(fl, 1, scale))) in the code. The warning of -Inf logProb sometimes appears but I do not think it is not a big concern here; it simply means the observation is impossible given the parameter values.

Having a deeper look into your code, I found one thing that does not make perfect sense to me. In your code for the covariate forklength (fl) you have the following code:
for (t in (first[i]+1):nyears) {
      delta[i, t] ~ dgamma(a, b)
      fl[i, t] <- fl[i, t-1] + delta[i, t]
}
This looks like a diffusion process for modelling the revolution of the time-varying covariate which is not observable if the individual is not captured. Say, if one individual is first captured on occasion 1 (say fl = 5) and then not captured on occasion 2 (fl missing) but captured on occasion 3 (say fl=6). So your fl vector for this individual will be (5, NA, 6). You only need one delta element here to model the single missing value, but it seems like you have got two. Your code will modify fl[3] but this is an observation, not missing. You will need to set up correctly when you need delta to model the missing covariate, I think.

Best wishes,
Wei

